Port - is a 16-bit number(0-65535) that identifies a specific service on a machine.
If an IP address is like a building's street address, a port is the apartment number
it tells traffic exactly which service to go to.

Three port ranges:

0-1023 -> Well-known ports (reserved, require root/admin)
1024-49151 -> Registered ports (common apps, IANA-registered)
49152-65535 -> Ephemeral ports (temporary, used by clients)

Essential ports to know:

80 HTTP
443 HTTPS
8080 HTTP alternative(dev servers, proxies)
8443 HTTPS alternative
3000 Common dev server (React, Node, Rails)

25 SMTP -> Server-to-server mail transfer
587 SMTP -> Client to mail server(submission)
465 SMTPS -> SMTP over TLS
110 POP3 -> Download mail(older)
995 POP3S -> POP3 over TLS
143 IMAP -> Sync mail across devices
993 IMAPS -> IMAP over TLS

22 SSH -> Secure remote shell
23 Telnet -> Unencrypted shell(obsolete, never use)
3389 RDP -> Windows Remote Desktop

20 FTP data
21 FTP control
22 SFTP -> FTP over SSH(same port as SSH)
69 TFTP -> Trivial FTP, UDP, no auth

53 DNS -> UDP for queries, TCP for zone transfers
67 DHCP -> Server(assigns IP addresses)
68 DHCP -> Client
123 NTP -> Time synchronization

3306 MySQL/MariaDB
5432 PostgreSQL
1433 Microsoft SQL Server
1521 Oracle DB
27017 MongoDB
6379 Redis

21 FTP
389 LDAP -> Directory services
636 LDAPS -> LDAP over TLS
5601 Kibana
9200 Elasticsearch
2181 Zookeeper
9092 Kafka
